  - rank: 6
    decision_ref: decisions/006-runtime-dependence.yawn
    leverage: 26.4
    split: genuinely-split
    question: "Must a .yawn stay readable and checkable with nothing installed, or is it enough that the record reads with nothing while checking it needs Node and ajv and running it needs Dave's Windows desktop — and is that three-way split written anywhere?"
    why: "The written rule promises a boring portable folder that works with no software, while the only proof that anything holds is a Node 22 test suite with one npm dependency, and the four scheduled automations run PowerShell against Dave's OneDrive desktop paths that appear 32 times across 12 tracked records. Once answered, every child Yawn and bot below Dave inherits an explicit answer to what it may depend on at each layer, instead of copying whichever absolute path or interpreter it finds nearest."
    candidates:
      - name: A
        description: "rule: The protocol runs on a named stack. Records may name absolute workspace paths, PowerShell scripts and vendor tools; package.json declares engines; the v0_lock entries 'no required parser' and 'no required UI' are struck from core/design-principles.yawn and the yawn-space rule is narrowed to the record text only."
        recommended: false
      - name: B
        description: "rule: Zero-dependency portability at every layer. Every record reads with nothing; every check runs on any host with Node alone (ajv vendored or dropped, both .ps1 scripts ported to .mjs); no tracked record carries a drive-letter or OneDrive path — workspaces are `workspace_ref:` names resolved from an untracked local file; principals are roles (principal:dave), never a hard-coded address."
        recommended: false
      - name: C
        description: "rule: Three layers, each written where it binds. (1) Reading: a .yawn record needs nothing installed — the v0 lock stays. (2) Checking: conformance needs the pinned toolchain declared in package.json engines and named in scripts/node.yawn, whose purpose changes from 'dependency-free checks' to 'one pinned toolchain, no runtime authority'; ajv is the one declared dependency. (3) Operating: an automation needs an operator workspace, written as `workspace_ref:` and resolved outside the tree; no tracked record carries a drive-letter path. The two PowerShell scripts are either ported or marked operator-only under layer 3 and excluded from layer 2's promise."
        recommended: true
    seal:
      question: decisions/006-runtime-dependence.yawn
      axis: runtime-dependence
      predicted_answer: "C"
      predicted_reason: "The authored side is unanimous: eleven authored judgments for portability, none for binding — Dave has stated the preference and the six binding judgments are all REVEALED readings of what the running system does. But pure B is falsified by the thing that proves everything else: the passing suite is Node plus ajv, and J0314's own note records that ajv is the one devDependency. A rule that says 'no required parser' while the only proof requires one is the kind of stated-but-not-honoured rule this repository says it does not want. C keeps the authored promise exactly where it was made (reading a record) and writes the two unwritten exceptions down where they already operate. GROUND_TRUTH facts 19 and 20 corroborate that the operator layer is where the invariant breaks (the workspace root every scheduled bot runs against is an uppercase-extension path the validator cannot see); validate-proof-refs.mjs already implements C's mechanism for proof blocks with a ledger of two. Leaning C over B mainly on cost; if Dave is willing to port both scripts and vendor ajv, B is the cleaner rule."
      confidence: 0.7
      model: "decisions/006-runtime-dependence.yawn recommendation, attributed agent-on-behalf, authority none; no provider call"
      t_sealed: "2026-09-12T00:00:00Z"
      blind: false
      source_refs: [decisions/006-runtime-dependence.yawn]
      hash: sha256:3a9b1b2ae47b666165ab199d1916115bf4921165aaac459f49a4e56b5181de23
  - rank: 7
    decision_ref: decisions/007-closed-until-opened.yawn
    leverage: 26
    split: leaning
    question: "When a record, template or gate says nothing, is the answer no until someone opens it — and does that rule also cover the values a template ships prefilled, like reversible: true, status: accepted, or a consent list that starts with yes?"
    why: "The written rule (capability plane zero-exposure, four authority flags false, capture locked first) is deny-by-default, but four templates and one root record quietly ship the opposite, and every record copied from them inherits an authorization nobody gave. Once answered, a bot that copies templates/observation.yawn or interface/move.yawn knows whether the values it inherits are permissions or blanks it must earn."
    candidates:
      - name: A
        description: "rule: Nothing is permitted by default. Every field that carries authority meaning (authorized, consent, follow_up_allowed, status, confidence, capability exposure, reversible) ships empty or at its most conservative value in every template and question output block; a permissive value exists only where an author wrote it and can be asked why."
        recommended: false
      - name: B
        description: "rule: A field is open unless a record closes it. Templates may prefill permissive values; root documents govern by enumerating prohibitions, and anything not prohibited is allowed."
        recommended: false
      - name: C
        description: "rule: Nothing is permitted by default, EXCEPT reversible, which may ship true because reversibility grants nothing (questions/what-can-i-do-next.yawn boundary: 'Reversibility does not silently grant authorization') — that sentence is copied next to every prefilled reversible. templates/observation.yawn ships status: draft and no confidence; codex-t4o-parser lists unknown first like email-intake; agency-declaration.yawn move.authorized: true is grandfathered by name as the principal's own published act and gains selected_by: principal:dave."
        recommended: true
    seal:
      question: decisions/007-closed-until-opened.yawn
      axis: default-authorisation-posture
      predicted_answer: "C"
      predicted_reason: "All five authored judgments sit on deny-by-default and the only test-enforced behaviour on this axis is on that side too (interception governance: 'the recurring contract can propose but cannot generate, spend, or publish'; 'approval is one-time ... never recurring authority'). Every permit-by-default judgment is revealed, and four of six are template prefill artifacts nobody argued for. reversible: true is the one exception with an authored reason sitting beside it in the same file, so it is worth writing down rather than removing; the observation template's accepted/confidence 1 is the sharpest violation and the in-flight status-vocabulary test already lists it as legacy, so C is mostly finishing work the tree has started."
      confidence: 0.7
      model: "decisions/007-closed-until-opened.yawn recommendation, attributed agent-on-behalf, authority none; no provider call"
